        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1585" ANNOTATION_REF="ann0">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>The sago palm thatch is a thing that we use to build houses here in the Teop region.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1586" ANNOTATION_REF="ann3">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>It is put on top of the house to protect (it) against the rain, the sun and the storm.  The work on the sago palm is done as follows:</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1587" ANNOTATION_REF="ann6">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When we have arrived at the place where the sago palms are, we must carefully choose one that is fully mature and also has many branches.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1588" ANNOTATION_REF="ann9">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When we have chosen (it), we fell it or we climb it.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1589" ANNOTATION_REF="ann12">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>As for the climbing, we climb it, and then we only cut the branches on the top, while the sago palm is still standing.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1590" ANNOTATION_REF="ann15">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>As for the felling, we fell the sago palm so that it lies on the ground, while we are cutting off the branches.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1591" ANNOTATION_REF="ann18">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>As for the cutting of branches, we must also be selective, so that we won't get the branches that do not look good, branches that are torn or that look young and  moist.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1592" ANNOTATION_REF="ann21">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When we have cut the sago palm branches, we now go on to do the work of cutting off the leaves, we now cut the sago-palm leaves off their branch.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1593" ANNOTATION_REF="ann24">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When this part is finished, we now go on to do the work of stacking up the sago palm leaves.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1594" ANNOTATION_REF="ann27">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When stacking (them) up, we stack up five or six sago palm leaves.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1597" ANNOTATION_REF="ann36">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When the work of stacking up is finished, we make one load. Then we tie it up by tying up three bundles, (and) then we carry (it) home.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1598" ANNOTATION_REF="ann39">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>A single person can cut off the leaves of four branches.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1599" ANNOTATION_REF="ann42">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Now, when we have arrived in the village, we do the next work, the work of tearing.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1600" ANNOTATION_REF="ann45">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>As for the tearing, we tear the midrib off the sago palm leaf, so that it is flexible when we are sewing (the thatch sheets).</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1601" ANNOTATION_REF="ann48">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When we have finished the tearing, we go on to do the work of stacking them (the leaves) up again, (and) then we fold them.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1602" ANNOTATION_REF="ann51">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>but we must fold (them) according to one measure.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1603" ANNOTATION_REF="ann54">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When the folding is finished, we put them together, one on top of the other, then we tie them up again.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1604" ANNOTATION_REF="ann57">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>We carefully lay them in a place that is completely dry.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1605" ANNOTATION_REF="ann60">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>They will lie there, while we are waiting for the sewing day.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1606" ANNOTATION_REF="ann63">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>We can now directly sew (the thatch), when the kururu bamboo strip (on which the leaves are sewn) is prepared, or when the group of workers is big.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1607" ANNOTATION_REF="ann66">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>But many days (the bundles) must lie (there) before (we can start).</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1608" ANNOTATION_REF="ann69">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>As for the work of sewing, (it) is done as follows:</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1609" ANNOTATION_REF="ann72">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>We put the bamboo strip on the ground, then we open a sago-palm leaf, because we had folded it before.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1610" ANNOTATION_REF="ann75">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Then we put the bamboo strip inside the (folded) sago palm leaf.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1611" ANNOTATION_REF="ann78">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Then we cover it up again.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1612" ANNOTATION_REF="ann81">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Then we stick the koopu bamboo (needle) from above into the upper part of the (folded) sago-palm thatch sheet.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1613" ANNOTATION_REF="ann84">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Then we insert the midrib that we have torn off the sago palm leaf when doing the sago palm leaf tearing.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1614" ANNOTATION_REF="ann87">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>We push it into (the hole of the folded leaf), and then we push it out (of the folded leaf).</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1615" ANNOTATION_REF="ann90">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Then we put another leaf (on the bamboo strip) and then go on sewing.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1616" ANNOTATION_REF="ann93">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>We will do it continuously just like this until the strip is used up. </ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1617" ANNOTATION_REF="ann96">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>With a nin reed (instead of a bamboo strip), it is just the same.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1618" ANNOTATION_REF="ann99">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>But (if) there is not any bamboo, we insert the nin reed,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1619" ANNOTATION_REF="ann102">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>because it is flexible, and we carve a sharp pointed end that we push into the leaf. </ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1620" ANNOTATION_REF="ann105">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When the work of sewing is finished, we do the next work of putting up the thatch.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1621" ANNOTATION_REF="ann108">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Before putting up the thatch, we make a scaffold, a place on which we stand, when we are putting up the thatch.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1622" ANNOTATION_REF="ann111">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And when thatching, we can thatch upwards (starting from the bottom) or thatch downwards (starting from the ridgepole).</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1623" ANNOTATION_REF="ann114">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Two people would do the work of reaching up the thatch sheets, while others would fix them. </ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1624" ANNOTATION_REF="ann117">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>We tie (a thatch sheet) onto the rafter with the sareai vine, the nin reed or the calamus cane.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1625" ANNOTATION_REF="ann120">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When the thatch sheet lies on the rafters, we tie it (the string) around the rafters.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1626" ANNOTATION_REF="ann123">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When it is finished, we reach up another (thatch sheet), until the end of the rafters is reached. </ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1627" ANNOTATION_REF="ann126">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When the thatching moves upwards, we put one sheet of sago palm thatch on top of the other, until the ridgepole is reached.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1628" ANNOTATION_REF="ann129">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When the thatching moves downwards, we start at the ridgepole, and then it goes down,  when we shove one sago palm thatch sheet underneath the other.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1629" ANNOTATION_REF="ann132">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When one side of the house is finished, we start with the other side.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1630" ANNOTATION_REF="ann135">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>When it is finished, this (thatching) is finished now, and we put the ridge sheet over this open space where the (two sides of the) thatch meet, this is (on top of) the upper ridge pole.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
    <TIER DEFAULT_LOCALE="de" LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="f" PARENT_REF="free transl." PARTICIPANT="unknown" TIER_ID="lit. transl.">
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1640" ANNOTATION_REF="a1585">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>(lit. The sago palm thatch, it (is) another thing that we make houses with here in Teop.)</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1634" ANNOTATION_REF="a1586">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>(lit. It (is) what they put on top of the house ...)</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1641" ANNOTATION_REF="a1602">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>(but we must fold (and) follow one measure.)</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1635" ANNOTATION_REF="a1611">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>(lit. Then we lay (it) flat by putting it on top of it.)</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1636" ANNOTATION_REF="a1616">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>(lit. We will  do it continously just like this, then it comes out where the strip ends.)</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1638" ANNOTATION_REF="a1619">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>(lit. and we sharpen the pushing part)</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1639" ANNOTATION_REF="a1620">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE></ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <REF_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1637" ANNOTATION_REF="a1626">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE> (lit. ... so that it arrives where the rafters stop.)</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </REF_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
    <LINGUISTIC_TYPE GRAPHIC_REFERENCES="false" LINGUISTIC_TYPE_ID="ref_id" TIME_ALIGNABLE="true"/>
